<LearningGuideLayout title="How to Add Physics Objects in Blender for Godot" description="Configure Blender objects for Godot physics with FPE mass, friction, bounciness, collision, and continuous collision detection settings." summary="Physics is where a pretty prop starts feeling like part of the world. A mug should settle, a rubber ball should bounce, and a heavy crate should resist being shoved. Author those material expectations beside the object itself." relatedSlugs={["holdable-items-godot","build-levels-in-blender-for-godot","interactive-objects-godot"]} fpeHeading="What FPE creates in Godot">
    <div class="guide-summary"><strong>You will make:</strong> a Blender prop that imports as a physical Godot object with intentional weight, grip, bounce, and collision behavior.</div>
    <h2>Prepare the object</h2><ol><li>Work at real-world scale and apply scale before judging physics.</li><li>Set the object's origin where rotation and balance make sense.</li><li>Use simple collision intent whenever possible. Detailed display geometry rarely needs equally detailed collision.</li><li>Open <strong>Item → Folded Paper Engine</strong> and check <strong>Physics</strong>.</li></ol>
    <h2>Describe how the prop should feel</h2><ul><li><strong>Mass:</strong> controls inertia. Start with a plausible value and compare related props rather than chasing perfect simulation.</li><li><strong>Friction:</strong> increase it for grippy crates and reduce it for slippery objects.</li><li><strong>Bounciness:</strong> keep ordinary furniture low; raise it deliberately for balls or playful props.</li><li><strong>Continuous Collision Detection:</strong> enable it for small or fast objects that could otherwise pass through thin colliders.</li></ul>
    <h2>Export a tiny physics test</h2><ol><li>Place the prop above a collidable floor.</li><li>Export with <strong>Custom Properties</strong> enabled.</li><li>Load the GLB through FPE and let the object fall.</li><li>Check resting orientation, sliding, bounce, tunneling, and scale.</li><li>Change one property at a time in Blender and re-export until the result communicates the material you imagined.</li></ol>
    <h2>Common problems</h2><ul><li><strong>Explodes or spins wildly:</strong> inspect overlapping collision, origin placement, scale, and spawn position.</li><li><strong>Falls through the floor:</strong> verify both objects have collision; use continuous detection only when speed or size justifies it.</li><li><strong>Everything feels weightless:</strong> check scale first, then compare mass and damping in Godot.</li><li><strong>Imported edits disappear:</strong> fix authored physics in Blender or a stable extension, not inside the generated scene.</li></ul>
    <Fragment slot="fpe"><p>FPE uses the Blender Physics checkbox and settings to construct the corresponding Godot rigid-body behavior and collider support. You can combine a physical prop with <strong>Holdable</strong> for first-person carrying. Engineers can then extend forces, damage, constraints, or special reactions without making artists repeat basic body setup after every export.</p><p><a href="/docs/fpe-physics-and-colliders.html">Read the FPE physics and collider reference</a>.</p></Fragment>
</LearningGuideLayout>
